<!--
    kuna infrastructure test: DWARF discriminant-guarded variant selection
    (`option variantguard`).

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 value+type facts, aggregate-union (which union field
                       wins).
    P0 assertion:      option variantguard on|off  (default on).

    `dwarfvariants` (DIV-87) imports a Rust tagged enum's `DW_TAG_variant_part`
    and installs it as a struct of a tag plus a union of one payload struct per
    variant, then deliberately refuses to NAME a variant wherever two of them
    overlay one byte range: a union member selects itself by OFFSET and the
    discriminant is never consulted, so `Ok.__0` and `Err.__0` (both at offset 8,
    both 8 bytes wide) tie in `ScoreUnionFields` and the winner is trial order.
    Every `Result` is that case, so its payload is spelled `field_0x8` on every
    access.

    `variantguard` is the dominating-guard analysis that side table was recorded
    for. It seeds from the DWARF discriminant (a CBRANCH testing the tag bytes
    against a constant, or a constant store over them), propagates the answer
    over the block graph, and reaches a value the compiler HOISTED above the
    branch through a backward walk over def-use. The printer then spells the
    `DW_TAG_variant` name where, and only where, that proof exists.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Every pass clears it
    and loads a real x86-64 ELF fixture, because a `bytechunk` carries no DWARF
    at all. Unlike `dwarfvariants`, this gate is a plain `Architecture` flag, so
    the `option` line comes AFTER `load file` (a `clear architecture` would drop
    it).

    Passes 1 and 2 (`dwarfvariants_overlay_x86_64`) are the CONSUMER side:
      * `use16` matches on the Result and returns `v` from the Ok arm and
        `e + 100` from the Err arm. Off, BOTH arms render
        `v2.payload.field_0x8`. On, the arm guarded by `!(v2.tag & 1)`, i.e.
        discriminant 0, which DWARF says is `Ok`, names `Ok`; and the `+ 100`
        arm, which rustc computed BEFORE the branch and selected with a cmov,
        names `Err`.
      * `put_res` is the REFUSAL: rustc computes the discriminant branchlessly
        (`dst->tag = (x < 0xb)`), so no constant ever reaches the tag and the
        payload store sits in a block that can still reach both variants. It
        must keep the offset spelling in BOTH passes.
      * `put_opt` is the second refusal: an `Option` has one payload-carrying
        variant, so `dwarfvariants` already names `Some` and this pass never
        considers the layout at all. Identical in both passes.

    Passes 3 and 4 (`dwarfvariants_x86_64`) are the PRODUCER side:
      * `ret_multi` is an enum with a struct variant P and a tuple variant Q
        whose payloads overlay at offset 8. Each arm stores a literal
        discriminant, so on, the facets are named `P` and `Q` instead of
        `field_0x4` and `field_0x8`. The FIELD inside the facet keeps its own
        suppression (`P.field_0x8`): that is the `dwarfvariants` per-field rule,
        which this pass does not touch.
      * `ret_three` is the third refusal: its two payload variants occupy
        DISJOINT ranges, so `dwarfvariants` already forces `A` and `B` and the
        layout is skipped. Identical in both passes.

    Passes 5 and 6 (`variantguard_clobber_x86_64`) are the PRODUCER-ORDERING
    rule, and they exist because the first revision of this pass got it wrong.
    A constant `tag = K` store is evidence about what the object BECOMES; it says
    nothing about a value read BEFORE it. That revision propagated the store
    backwards over whole blocks with no ordering test and no kill, so an
    UNGUARDED read of the caller's value came out `(dst->payload).Err.__0`.
      * `read_then_clobber` and `read_then_clobber_ok` are byte-identical up to
        the clobber constant, so the bug gave the SAME instruction opposite
        variant names. Both reads must now render by OFFSET, and the assertion
        counts them together so a future regression cannot pass by breaking only
        one of the pair.
      * `read_then_store` is the shape whose output contradicted itself three
        lines apart: named `Err`, then tested for `Ok` and returned unchanged.
      * `guard_then_clobber` is the coexistence case: the read IS proved, by the
        GUARD, so it names `Ok`, while the store below it names `Err`. Neither
        fact is allowed to decide the other.
      * The WRITES keep their names in every one of them, because that is what a
        producer store actually proves.

    The same rule has a GUARD side, added after a second review round. A guard
    proves what the object was AT THE GUARD; it does not survive a store over the
    tag bytes, a call that may store through the pointer, or any store this pass
    cannot attribute to the object at all (which may alias it). A revision that
    let a singleton guard region beat a correctly-computed producer fact by
    PRECEDENCE named a write that builds `Err`, inside an `Ok`-guarded block,
    `Ok` , one line below its own `tag = 1`. The two facts are now INTERSECTED
    and a disagreement refuses.
      * `guarded_write` and `guarded_write_mirror` are that shape and its mirror:
        the guard-proved read keeps its name, and the write below names the
        variant it actually builds.
      * `stale_read` is the same with no call at all: the tag is overwritten
        between the branch and the load.
      * `read_after_call_ok_guard` and `read_after_call_err_guard` are the guard
        side's CONTROL PAIR, the analogue of read_then_clobber/_ok: identical but
        for which arm the guard selects and which variant the call writes, so the
        same load must not get opposite names. Both must render by offset.
      * `alias_clobber` writes the payload of `p` after a store through `q` that
        may be the same object; that write is refused while the two writes this
        pass CAN attribute keep their names.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
